#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161689 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f1ec99-e166-5986-a07d-f07be0dfeb31" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "70991204-370a-5804-a04e-7e664db052de" :question/math-academy-id "e-8048" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071806/assets/1d7b89b6790ed713a8885b5124d988cda34cccff589ceaef9d556708bc21233f.png)\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\n- $R_1 := \\ldots$\n\n- $R_3:=R_3-3R_2$\n\n- $R_4:= \\ldots$\n\neach of the form $R_i:=R_i+kR_2,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries above and below $a_{22}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $R_1 := R_1 - 2R_2\\quad$ and $\\quad R_4:=R_4+2R_2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161690 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "958cdcf3-aaba-5275-85f8-9531f10b3d0e" :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "cbf8e85c-ffa6-55b9-a087-2a5272f10f25" :question/math-academy-id "e-8049" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071806/assets/81176c8bb51128cdaf5d72842e2b2b909b1c41d63249eb3640d745bc9e0c7f9b.png)\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\n- $C_1:=\\ldots$\n\n- $C_3:= C_3 - 4C_2$\n\n- $C_4:= \\ldots$\n\neach of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_2,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of $a_{32}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 7 & -2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -3 & -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+5{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -3 & -1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -23 & -3 & -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-4{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -3 & 11 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -23 & -3 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-2{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -3 & 11 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -23 & -3 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $C_1:=C_1+5C_2\\quad$ and $\\quad C_4:=C_4-2C_2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161691 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908b96db-9463-50b5-89a3-056072c930ba" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "399b7598-c793-5032-9f33-ac02c7b85fe1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8050" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071806/assets/572d87625c3056d795203d2592c72aa063d1e125d386365ea620b4b596869085.png)\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_i:=R_i+kR_1,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below $a_{11}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $R_2:=R_2-2R_1\\quad$ and $\\quad R_3:=R_3+3R_1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161692 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85e1fe3f-e85e-5ab4-a594-c55b84bb789a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "f9912357-1d1d-5413-917e-257dc5dfc0fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-8051" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071806/assets/941de9bd4837b4b5c9f0d2bf64c4ea0fd9be657b0368d03cac169742083822d1.png)\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_3,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of $a_{13}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+6{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+5{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -16 & -17 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $C_1:=C_1+6C_3\\quad$ and $\\quad C_2:=C_2+5C_3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908b96db-9463-50b5-89a3-056072c930ba" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b8c1eae-857c-5731-8285-91d6f4afe9dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373380} {:answer/id #uuid "c0459213-0b6c-5853-a3c3-fe1a564f35d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373381} {:answer/id #uuid "fc48bf2d-7f94-563e-84b8-38654a77deda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373382} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c8e53e-72f8-569c-9340-0493c4ee85af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373383} {:answer/id #uuid "998870eb-7619-55c8-bf90-f1026375a37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "998870eb-7619-55c8-bf90-f1026375a37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373384} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1d2c21d-93cf-5c6e-9f64-17ddcfb69439" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc4fe478-ecca-5d83-9a4b-47e34a0bc1c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-130448" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below ${a}_{12}=-2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908b96db-9463-50b5-89a3-056072c930ba" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6be4ae64-a256-5962-8838-7010c8796db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373387} {:answer/id #uuid "db49b698-4c11-5e23-a25e-1c2ec8662450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373388} {:answer/id #uuid "05d62c28-3fc6-5415-b059-6f59b5077c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373389} {:answer/id #uuid "904bef2e-7d4a-52e4-8984-e824864b775a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373390} {:answer/id #uuid "11f2b5f1-d225-5660-83d5-7248cb2335f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db49b698-4c11-5e23-a25e-1c2ec8662450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373388} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dbaba08-423f-525c-a9ec-822cfb4c2ccb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe193bd2-0603-54d6-a24f-8d894be1f810" :question/math-academy-id "q-130451" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above ${a}_{33}=-3$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f1ec99-e166-5986-a07d-f07be0dfeb31" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fdc6f03-dff5-5552-9765-34d24f5fe9a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373394} {:answer/id #uuid "7f54cd25-1e53-5a88-8deb-f986bc17ad1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373395} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc1c691-4b1f-542f-bcf2-e9262b187cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373396} {:answer/id #uuid "574d5061-5c55-5862-bb62-3a8cd36a5f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373397} {:answer/id #uuid "44a6af6f-cb87-529b-a3b4-d09793748a59" :answer/type {:db